WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:04.670
So I recently discovered this box full of
childhood memorabilia.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:09.080
It hasn't been explored in at least 10 years,
and today we're going to find out what's inside.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.790
The first thing I notice when I open the box
is this participant badge to a science and

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:14.790
engineering fair.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:18.490
I didn't finish first, I didn't finish second,
or third, but I did participate.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:23.359
Hank, the fact that mom and dad chose to keep
my participant ribbon perhaps indicates why

00:00:23.359 --> 00:00:25.760
you're the brother who deconstructed an adding
machine.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.170
Here I have a "mom and dad why did you ever
let me buy those glasses?"

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:30.170
picture.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:32.660
Photograph of me from my brief experimentation
with contact lenses.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:35.720
And a picture of me from back when I was the
worst player on my childhood soccer team.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.720
Alright what else?

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:37.720
Oh Hank look!

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.790
Academic Decathlon Medals.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:43.250
So Hank it looks like the rest of this box
contains boxes.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:45.890
When I was a kid I had a, uh, box collection?

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:48.460
And by "when I was a kid" I mean until my
early twenties.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.559
Anyway, lets see if the boxes have anything
in them.

00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:53.310
This is a lovely box given to me by my college
girlfriend.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.000
It's empty, that's a metaphor.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.000
This ones a winner.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.350
Oh and there's stuff inside!

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.440
But sadly it's just more boxes.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:01.440
Empty box.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:02.440
Another empty box.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.120
What childhood memories did you preserve John?

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.880
Oh, I preserved memories of emptiness.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.520
This box actually has something in it; it
is a rose given to me by Betsy Hinckle, my

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.520
tenth-grade home-coming dance date.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.479
Oh my god there is also a tooth in there!

00:01:17.479 --> 00:01:20.650
It is one of my baby teeth Hank, or at any
rate, someone's baby teeth.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:24.459
This box had some of my old cub scout patches
in it from 1985.

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:26.939
This box had a stamp from Germany inside of
it.

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:28.689
Glad I chose to keep that.

00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:29.689
This one's empty.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:33.679
This one I can't figure out how to open, that's
kind of exciting.

00:01:33.679 --> 00:01:34.859
GAHHH!

00:01:34.859 --> 00:01:35.859
It's empty.

00:01:35.859 --> 00:01:36.859
This box just has a bunch of pennies in it.

00:01:36.859 --> 00:01:38.160
I don't know why I chose to keep them.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:39.909
You shouldn't even exist pennies!

00:01:39.909 --> 00:01:43.310
I know what you're wondering Hank, what's
in this box?

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.659
You will never guess, unless you guessed boxes.

00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:51.399
Alright Hank, last box, my most beloved box
from childhood -- this real cigar box, let's

00:01:51.399 --> 00:01:52.590
see what's inside.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:53.979
Oh, there's pictures.

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:56.809
There is actually something of value in here!

00:01:56.809 --> 00:01:59.509
These are not pictures of me.

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:01.320
These are mostly picture of flowers.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.320
Flower, flower...

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.320
No!

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.719
Oh, there's me, What am I doing?

00:02:05.719 --> 00:02:09.450
Am I eating a snake?

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:12.980
That's the one Hank, that's the one picture
of me I chose to keep.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:17.900
Hank, down at the bottom beneath all of the
boxes, was this beautiful book, "Baby's milestones,

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.800
birth to seven years" filled out by our mom
and dad.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.510
There is like a little bracelet that Mom wore
at the hospital.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.629
Polaroid pictures of me just after birth.

00:02:25.629 --> 00:02:29.430
Here are my foot prints, and here on this
page, Hank, preserved for all time my Mom

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:35.410
and Dad's first words about me my Mother said
"He's ugly" exclamation point.

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:39.000
And my Dad said, "He's so purple" exclamation
point.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.409
Ah, to be fair I have seen a few newborns
in my day.

00:02:41.409 --> 00:02:43.180
They do tend towards the ugly and the purple.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.170
So Hank, what have we learned from this trip
down memory lane?

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:48.069
Two things I think, first, put things in your
boxes.

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:53.010
And secondly life is not so much about things
as it is about people, and what we do together.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.340
Hank, the amazing thing about that box of
childhood memories is that you could go through

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.989
the whole thing without ever knowing I had
a brother.

00:02:58.989 --> 00:03:03.519
And yet nonetheless it is completely impossible
for me to imagine my life without you, you

00:03:03.519 --> 00:03:05.439
adding-machine deconstructing nerd.

00:03:05.439 --> 00:03:06.709
Hank, I will see you on Friday.

